{"ex-503-1":{"number":"1","title":"Fission of plutonium","question":"<p>A plutonium-239 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pu</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) nucleus is bombarded by a neutron and undergoes fission. It splits into a molybdenum-104 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Mo</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) nucleus and a tellurium-134 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Te</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) nucleus, releasing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> neutrons.</p><p>(a) Write an equation for the reaction in terms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Hence, find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) If there is enough Pu-239 fuel, is it possible for this reaction to trigger a chain reaction? Explain.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pu</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup>n → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Mo</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Te</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> <sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup>n</p><p>Consider the mass numbers.</p><p>239 + 1 = 104 + 134 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × 1</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2</p><p>(b) Yes, because more than one neutron is produced in each fission, which can trigger further fissions.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 503  |  Source PDF p. 4","source":{"name":"503 Examples.docx","sha256":"0ba1c5ee7db6780312d9b1738d332725d0695d7a590217cc0a8e2cc85bb35992","block":2},"id":"ex-503-1","chapter":"503"},"ex-503-2":{"number":"2","title":"Mass-energy relationship","question":"<p>The amount of energy released by the Sun in each second is 3.85 × 10<sup>26</sup> J and the energy is converted from the mass of the Sun. Find the mass lost</p><p>by the Sun every month. Take 1 month = 2.59 × 10<sup>6</sup> s.</p>","answer":"<p>Consider a period of 1 month.</p><p>Energy released Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (3.85 × 10<sup>26</sup>) × (2.59 × 10<sup>6</sup>) = 9.97 × 10<sup>32</sup> J</p><p>By Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>,</p><p>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Δ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.11 × 10<sup>16</sup> kg</p><p>The Sun loses 1.11 × 10<sup>16</sup> kg every month.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 503  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"503 Examples.docx","sha256":"0ba1c5ee7db6780312d9b1738d332725d0695d7a590217cc0a8e2cc85bb35992","block":13},"id":"ex-503-2","chapter":"503"},"ex-503-3":{"number":"3","title":"Units of mass and energy","question":"<p>The mass of a uranium-235 nucleus is 234.993 422 u.</p><p>(a) Express the mass in kg.</p><p>(b) Hence find the amount of energy equivalent to this mass. Express your answer in J.</p><p>(c) Find the amount of energy equivalent to 1 kg of uranium-235. Express your answer in MeV.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Mass = 234.993 422 × (1.661 × 10<sup>−27</sup>)</p><p>= 3.90 × 10<sup>−25</sup> kg</p><p>(b) Energy = (3.90 × 10<sup>−25</sup>) × (3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup>)<sup>2</sup></p><p>= 3.51 × 10<sup>−8</sup> J</p><p>(c) Energy = 1 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 931</p><p>= 5.61 × 10<sup>29</sup> MeV</p>","tips":"<p>Tip: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> gives the mass in u.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 503  |  Source PDF p. 11","source":{"name":"503 Examples.docx","sha256":"0ba1c5ee7db6780312d9b1738d332725d0695d7a590217cc0a8e2cc85bb35992","block":23},"id":"ex-503-3","chapter":"503"},"ex-503-4":{"number":"4","title":"Energy released in nuclear fission","question":"<p>Consider the following nuclear fission reaction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>U</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup>n → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Ba</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Kr</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + 2<sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup>n</p><p>Total mass before reaction: 236.002 087 u. Total mass after reaction: 235.809 305 u.</p><p>(a) Find the loss in mass in the reaction. Express your answer in u.</p><p>(b) Hence find the energy released in the reaction. Express your answer in J.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 236.002 087 − 235.809 305</p><p>= 0.192 782 u</p><p>(b) Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> = (0.192 782 × 1.661 × 10<sup>−27</sup>) × (3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup>)<sup>2</sup></p><p>= 2.88 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Convert the unit of Δm from u to kg before applying</p><p>ΔE = Δmc<sup>2</sup>.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 503  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"503 Examples.docx","sha256":"0ba1c5ee7db6780312d9b1738d332725d0695d7a590217cc0a8e2cc85bb35992","block":38},"id":"ex-503-4","chapter":"503"},"ex-503-5":{"number":"5","title":"Energy released in nuclear fusion","question":"<p>Consider the nuclear fusion reaction below:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup>n</p><p>The table shows the masses of these particles.</p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Particle</p></td><td><p>Mass / u</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Neutron</p></td><td><p>1.008 665</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Hydrogen-2 nucleus</p></td><td><p>2.013 553</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Hydrogen-3 nucleus</p></td><td><p>3.015 501</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Helium-4 nucleus</p></td><td><p>4.001 505</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>(a) Find the loss in mass in the reaction. Express your answer in u.</p><p>(b) Hence find the energy released in the reaction. Express your answer in MeV.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (2.013 553 + 3.015 501) − (4.001 505 + 1.008 665) = 0.018 884 u</p><p>(b) Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.018 884 × 931 = 17.6 MeV</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 503  |  Source PDF p. 14","source":{"name":"503 Examples.docx","sha256":"0ba1c5ee7db6780312d9b1738d332725d0695d7a590217cc0a8e2cc85bb35992","block":54},"id":"ex-503-5","chapter":"503"}}